# ── What the review established, read firsthand at the pinned tags ──────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ `spec.runStrategy` IS REQUIRED BY THE VALIDATING WEBHOOK, AND `running` IS DEPRECATED.
# pkg/virt-api/webhooks/validating-webhook/admitters/vms-admitter.go, validateRunStrategy: a spec with
# neither `running` nor `runStrategy` is refused with "RunStrategy must be specified", and one with both
# with "Running and RunStrategy are mutually exclusive. Note that Running is deprecated". So the field
# cannot be left out for another manager to own, which is what decided that the reconciler READS it
# off the object before it renders — values.yaml § runStrategy.
#
# ⚠ THE PRINTABLE STATUS IS FIRST-MATCH-WINS OVER AN ORDERED LIST, AND `ErrorUnschedulable` COMES
# BEFORE `Provisioning` AND `WaitingForVolumeBinding`. pkg/virt-controller/watch/vm/vm.go,
# setPrintableStatus: Terminating, Stopping, Migrating, Paused, Running, ErrorPvcNotFound,
# DataVolumeError, ErrorUnschedulable, Provisioning, WaitingForVolumeBinding, ErrImagePull,
# ImagePullBackOff, Starting, CrashLoopBackOff, Stopped, WaitingForReceiver. isVirtualMachineStatusUnschedulable
# is the VMI carrying PodScheduled=False with reason Unschedulable. That ordering is why a machine on
# a node without KVM reads `ErrorUnschedulable` and not `WaitingForVolumeBinding`, although its root
# claim is also unbound.
#
# ⚠ A DataVolume IN WaitForFirstConsumer OR PendingPopulation COUNTS AS READY FOR THE INSTANCE TO BE
# CREATED. vm.go, handleDataVolumes: `case cdiv1.Succeeded, cdiv1.WaitForFirstConsumer,
# cdiv1.PendingPopulation: continue`. The VMI controller then renders a TEMPORARY launcher pod
# (pkg/virt-controller/watch/vmi/lifecycle.go, "Scheduling temporary pod for WaitForFirstConsumer
# DV", RenderLaunchManifestNoVm) to trigger the binding — and that pod requests
# `devices.kubevirt.io/kvm` exactly as the real one does (services/template.go,
# WithVirtualizationResources(getRequiredResources(vmi, …, AllowEmulation()))). On a node without
# the device the temporary pod is unschedulable, the claim never binds, the clone never runs, and the
# instance sits in `Pending` with `PodScheduled=False`; the VM reads `ErrorUnschedulable`.
#
# ⚠ THAT WAS THE STATE THIS CHART'S FIRST TEST WAS WRITTEN TO ASSERT, AND THE LANE HAS KVM. The record
# (charts/bundle/bundle.yaml § owed, `virtual-machines-need-a-node-with-kvm`; issue #95) said Docker
# Desktop's VM lends no /dev/kvm to a container. Measured on 2026-09-17: WSL2 runs with nested
# virtualization on, `docker run --privileged alpine ls -l /dev/kvm` answers `crw-rw---- 10, 232`, the
# k3s container is privileged, virt-handler's device plugin advertised `devices.kubevirt.io/kvm` on
# the node, and the first machine rendered from this chart went WaitingForVolumeBinding →
# Provisioning (the clone, ~20 s) → Running in 46 seconds. test/CyberCloud.Bundle.Cluster.Nightly
# § KubeVirtOnAnEmptyCluster asserts `Running`, the KVM device on the node and on the launcher pod's
# requests, and — since #28's review — that a charts/managed/disk claim the body names in `dataDisks`
# is populated once the machine consumes it and mounted by the launcher, and that
# VirtualMachines.Matches holds against the admitted object. The ErrorUnschedulable reading above is
# what a real node WITHOUT nested virtualization gives, and VirtualMachines.ReadinessOf carries the
# scheduler's sentence back for it.
#
# ⚠ THE NODE IS `kubevirt.io/schedulable=true` WITHOUT KVM TOO. pkg/virt-handler/heartbeat/heartbeat.go
# sets the label to false only while the device plugins are not yet initialised; a kvm plugin with
# no /dev/kvm initialises and advertises zero devices. So on such a node the scheduler's refusal is
# "Insufficient devices.kubevirt.io/kvm" on a node that otherwise accepts machines.
#
# ⚠ THE MUTATING WEBHOOK WRITES DEFAULTS INTO THE TEMPLATE — `architecture`, a machine type, the
# disk bus where none is given — so VirtualMachines.Matches is CONTAINMENT over the fields a tenant
# chose and never an equality over the spec.
#
# ⚠ WHAT THIS CHART DELIBERATELY DOES NOT RENDER, and where each is recorded: an instancetype
# (conformance.yaml § owed, `instancetypes-wait-for-the-bundles-catalogue`), a hotplug volume
# (charts/managed/disk, `hot-attach-is-not-rendered`), a VirtualMachineSnapshot (`snapshot-is-not-offered`),
# a second interface or a floating IP (`one-interface-and-no-floating-ip`).
upstream-webhook: kubevirt ships a MUTATING webhook for VirtualMachine (instancetype expansion, architecture, machine type, container-disk pull policy) and a VALIDATING one that requires runStrategy, checks every volume against its disk and refuses duplicate volume names; CDI ships a validating webhook for DataVolume that refuses a clone whose source claim is not there
